from gratipay.utils import markdown
[---]
banner = _("API")
title = _("Gratipay API")
[---] text/html
{% extends "templates/api.html" %}
{% block content %}{{ markdown.render("""

You've found the Gratipay API, version 2!


## How To Use This API

Most of the HTTP resources under [`/api/v2/`](/api/v2/) that you're browsing
right now as `text/html` also have JSON representations. Set the `Accept`
header to `application/json` to request the JSON version:

```bash
$ curl \"https://gratipay.com/api/v2/\" \\
    -H \"Accept: application/json\"
{\"you\": \"anon\"}
```


## Authentication

Use the user id and API key from [your settings page](/about/me/settings) to
authenticate your requests:

```bash
$ curl \"https://gratipay.com/api/v2/\" \\
    -H \"Accept: application/json\" \\
    -u \"$user_id:$api_key\"
{\"you\": \"your-username\"}
```


## Documentation

In addition to serving our JSON API, these pages also provide our HTML
documentation for Gratipay as a software application.

Conceptually speaking, Gratipay is an
[accounting](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accounting) application, with a
general ledger and chart of [accounts](/api/v2/accounts/) at its heart.
However, only admins ever interact with the ledger directly.  Normal ~users
interact with Gratipay by providing `payment_instructions` which we then
process during a weekly event called `payday`.

Here's a high-level technical overview of the software architecture by which we
deliver our application:

 - **[Database](/api/v2/database/)**&mdash;We store our data in [PostgreSQL
   9.3](http://www.postgresql.org/docs/9.3/static/), and we write a fair amount
of the [Postgres dialect of
SQL](http://www.postgresql.org/docs/9.3/static/sql.html). We use and maintain
the [postgres.py](http://postgres-py.readthedocs.org/) client library.

 - **Website**&mdash;The main [Gratipay.com](https://gratipay.com/) website
 (hosted at [Heroku](https://dashboard.heroku.com)) includes UI, API, documentation,
 and admin. This hopefully makes it easier for you to [get up and running
     locally](https://github.com/gratipay/gratipay.com#quick-start): install
 one app, and you have everything you need to hack on Gratipay all in one
 place. We use and maintain our own server-side web framework,
 [Aspen](http://aspen.io/), which uses [Python
     2](https://docs.python.org/release/2.7.9/). For the front-end, we use the
 SCSS syntax for [Sass](http://sass-lang.com/), [jQuery 1](https://jquery.com/), and
 [Backbone](http://backbonejs.org/) (for the admin dashboard). We route asset
 requests through [MaxCDN](https://www.maxcdn.com/) in production, with the
 main web app as the origin server. Fonts come from [H&Co](http://www.typography.com/)
 and [IcoMoon](https://icomoon.io/).

 - **Images**&mdash;We run a stateless image processor called
 [`gip.rocks`](http://gip.rocks) (hosted at
     [Heroku](https://dashboard.heroku.com)), for use in both production and
     development. Postgres stores the images and the main website serves them.

 - **Widgets**&mdash;We have a static file server at
   [`grtp.co`](https://grtp.co/) (hosted at [Digital
       Ocean](https://www.digitalocean.com/)) for serving widgets.

 - **Payday**&mdash;We [process
         payments](http://inside.gratipay.com/howto/run-payday) on a weekly
 cycle, using a combination of Python and Bash scripts.

 - **Email**&mdash;Both the website and payday send email notifications. We
   queue these up in Postgres, and the web app runs a thread for processing the
   queue. [Mandrill](https://mandrillapp.com/) is our email provider.

 - **Ops**&mdash;The website and payday processes file error reports to
   [Sentry](https://beta.getsentry.com/gratipay/). We use
   [PagerDuty](https://gratipay.pagerduty.com/) for monitoring, and we have
   some stats on [a Librato dashboard](https://metrics.librato.com/share/dashboards/42hmoj9j).


## Resources

Here are the resources currently exposed in this API:

 - [`accounts/`](/api/v2/accounts/)&mdash;our chart of accounts, requires the
   `admin` role

""" )}}{% endblock %}
